::: {.doc-subject}

Add a star graph (hub-and-spoke) to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L7972-L8097){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_star_graph(
    n,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Star graph](../assets/graphs/add-star-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three star graphs side by side: a hub with three spokes for n=4, four spokes for n=5, and six spokes for n=7. The hub is joined to every other node and the spokes are not joined to each other, so n nodes give n - 1 edges." width="100%"}

Creates a central hub node connected to `n - 1` peripheral nodes. All edges connect the hub
to spokes; there are no edges between spokes, so `n` nodes always give exactly `n - 1`
edges. The hub is the first node created and the spokes follow it in order. In a directed
graph every edge points outward, from the hub to a spoke.

The new nodes are appended to whatever is already in the graph: nothing is cleared and no
edges are made to existing nodes. Star graphs are handy for:

- modeling hub-and-spoke systems (a server with clients, an airline hub, a broadcaster)
- building test graphs with one extremely central node, e.g. to sanity-check centrality
  measures
- assembling larger structures by attaching stars to existing nodes with `add_edge()`
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Total number of nodes to add, counting the hub (so `n - 1` spokes). Must be at least
    `2`; smaller values raise a `ValueError`.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node, e.g. `"server"`. If
    `None` (default), no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string (so `"1"`,
    `"2"`, ... in an empty graph). The hub gets the first number. A label that is already
    used raises `ValueError` before anything is added.
    If `False`, the nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge, e.g. `"connects"`. If
    `None` (default), no `rel` attribute is set.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `n=` is less than `2`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_wheel_graph`) 

:   Add a wheel graph.

[](`~add_path_graph`) 

:   Add a path graph.

[](`~add_complete_bipartite_graph`) 

:   Add a complete bipartite graph.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A star graph has one hub, the first node, joined to every other node, with no edges between
the others, so `n` nodes have `n - 1` edges. `type=` and `rel=` tag the new nodes and
edges:

::: {#1121c66d .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_star_graph(n=4, type="server", rel="link")

graph.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['1--2', '1--3', '1--4']
```
:::
:::


When the graph already has nodes, the numbering continues from them. Here the hub is
labelled `"2"`, since `"Gateway"` is node `1`, and an extra edge links the gateway to the
hub:

::: {#14346fe5 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph = (
    ec.Graph()
    .add_node(label="Gateway")
    .add_star_graph(n=4)
    .add_edge("Gateway", "2")
)

graph.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
['2--3', '2--4', '2--5', 'Gateway--2']
```
:::
:::


### A star and a path

A star and a path on seven nodes are both trees with six edges. In the star on the left
every two nodes are at most two edges apart. In the path on the right the ends are six
apart:

::: {#018fd358 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
star = ec.Graph().add_star_graph(n=7)
path = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(n=7)

star.diameter(), path.diameter()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
(2, 6)
```
:::
:::


